	)
	(text "No common-mode choke is used, since no dedicated EMC testing is\nplanned to calibrate its parameters, guaranteeing an actual \nimprovement of the CAN performance."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 97.79 68.326 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "2c6f928a-26c5-467e-b011-525cc9b05676")
	)
	(text "CAN Interface"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 40.894 200.66 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
				(thickness 0.4572)
				(bold yes)
			)
		)
		(uuid "355c430b-e6ca-4213-ad32-9f90a33b5821")
	)
	(text "The Human Side subsystems needs two main voltage levels, 5V and 3.3V. The 5V is needed as the main CAN transmission \nlevel and as the Steering Wheel smart sensor peripheral power supply. The latter needs 5V because it needs an \nhigher voltage than 3.3V, because it needs to generate a stable 3.3V via LDO on the Sensor board for the Analog\nCircuitry. The 3.3V supply is used as the main voltage level for the Human Side board, including the supply of \nnot negligible power consumption loads, such as the dmain display and the LEDs.\n\nBoth these voltage levels are generated from the main 12V vehicle supply, via DC/DC buck converters. The\nBuck is used becuase it allows to reach the best possible power efficiency (good becuase we consume the battery\nslower, and because we need to manage less heat dissipation). \nThe 3.3V could have been generated via LDO from the 5V rail, but because it is used to supply significant loads (with the\npossibility in future to expand the consumption of the whole subsystem), the DC/DC choice was the best. \n3,3V is not derivated from 5V in order to not \"cascade\" the efficiencies of the two DC/DC converters, which, \nfrom some preliminary analysis, would have resoulted in a worse efficiency of the 3.3V rail. "
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 294.894 175.768 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "3664ad37-3f15-4865-9afa-e394f90fa462")
	)
	(text "Speaker"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 360.68 15.748 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 2.286 2.286)
				(thickness 0.4572)
				(bold yes)
			)
		)
		(uuid "37783256-94b7-4c73-9e22-23379f97c265")
	)
	(text ""
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 208.788 28.448 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 13.462 13.462)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "3d08985a-1919-462a-b7ee-8e4746a6bd41")
	)
	(text "The chosen CAN transceiver uses a 5V supply for the bus interface \r\nand a 3.3V VIO supply for compatibility with the MCU logic levels.\r\nThe 5V supply provides more voltage headroom for the CAN output \r\nstage, making it easier to meet the required electrical and EMC \r\nperformance at a lower cost. In particular, it reduces the design\r\nconstraints on the internal circuitry compared to a transceiver \r\npowered entirely at 3.3V, reducing the component cost by a \r\nsignificant amount.\r\nAt the same time, the separate 3.3V VIO supply allows direct \r\ncommunication with the MCU without requiring additional level \r\nshifters."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 97.536 81.026 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "4013211d-8a47-45e4-bdfe-640757bc5caf")
	)
	(text "Reset Button"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 231.902 63.754 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 2.286 2.286)
				(thickness 0.4572)
				(bold yes)
			)
		)
		(uuid "4a522ca4-5432-4867-9518-2954c646f24f")
	)
	(text "The bus uses split termination with two 60.4 Ohm resistors and a 4.7nF capacitor \nconnected from their midpoint to GND. The two resistors provide approximately \n120 Ohm differential termination, while the capacitor creates a low-pass filtering\neffect for common-mode noise. \nDuring normal CAN communication, CANH and CANL move in opposite directions, so the \nmidpoint voltage remains approximately constant and the capacitor has\nlittle effect on the differential signal.\r\n\nA larger capacitor would provide stronger common-mode filtering, but could also \nintroduce problems under the fault situation when when one CAN line becomes \ndisconnected. In this case, the differential symmetry is lost and the capacitor \ncan affect the useful signal, introducing an RC delay that may prevent the signal \nfrom reaching the required voltage thresholds before the CAN sampling point. \nFor this reason, 4.7nF was selected instead of a much larger value, such as \n100nF, to obtain some common-mode filtering without unnecessarily \ndegrading signal integrity under fault conditions.\r\n\r\nThe termination resistors are sized considering possible short circuit fault to \nthe 12V main supply of one of the CAN lines, which should not result in fathal malfunctions.\n	P_MAX = (12V / (2*60.4))^2 * 60.4 = 0.596 W \n	--> Max power loss on each resitor\n	-->	Termination resistor should at least be rated for 1 W (and PCB should\n		be designed to handle the heat dissipation)"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 146.05 80.01 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "52d5a858-e225-42c1-bba8-7c865a20e85d")
	)
	(text "Main Voltage (12V) \nMonitor"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 278.384 61.976 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 2.286 2.286)
				(thickness 0.4572)
				(bold yes)
			)
		)
		(uuid "5406e0ae-e1a7-40f6-9ee4-0b839ca15a89")
	)
	(text "Speaker Interface"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 105.41 199.136 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
				(thickness 0.4572)
				(bold yes)
			)
		)
		(uuid "55b055d6-78cd-4653-a902-1bb6199309f3")
	)
	(text "Connectors"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 62.738 176.53 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 2.286 2.286)
				(thickness 0.4572)
				(bold yes)
			)
		)
		(uuid "57437221-207c-4a4b-93e2-5dd4c556dba0")
	)
	(text "No hardware low-pass filter is used, so button debouncing must be handled in software. \nThe buttons are placed directly on the carrier PCB, so significant noise problems, \nwhich could have justified a filter, are not expected.\n\nExternal 47k pull-down resistors are used to guarantee a defined logic level even during \nMCU reset. The static Power consumption for each button is:\n\n	P = (3.3V)^2 / 47k = 0.23 mW"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 18.796 25.4 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "5c28ec64-c9a1-443e-8a5e-0dd388bffef9")
	)
	(text "A Filter is placed on each amplifier output to attenuate high-frequency noise generated by the switching elements, while preserving the audio signal.\nBecause of the very low impedance of the Speaker (8 Ohm), an RC filter is not raccomanded, because of the non negligible power loss on the resistor. \nA feerite bead is used insted, because it guardantees a very small DC and low frequency resistance (reducing the power dissipation of the filter), while having \na significant impedance at high frequency, implementing the filtering operation. \n\nThe low frequency dissipation is (assuming DC resistance specified in the datasheet is also valid in audio band, up to 10kHz):\n	P_loss = Ploss = Irms^2*(2*0.04) = 10 mW\n	--> Losses are negligible in comparison to speaker consumption\n\nThe DC current rating of the ferrite (3A) is chosen in accordance to peak current consumed by the Speaker (500 mA), with some margin to not run into \nthermal problems.\n\nAs noted in the TI reference \"TI_Audio Amplifier Output Filtering\", the exact values of the filter elements (ferrite and capacitors) are defined empirically,\nin order to address the real response and behavior of the circuit on hand. \nAs first guess, the guidelines of this document and amplifier datasheet are followed, chosing a ferrite with impedance > 100 Ohm at high frequency (it was chosen\none with 300Ohm @ 100 MHz), and the capacitor value of 220pF. \n"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 306.578 87.884 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "60da3abd-b431-414a-8209-cb80423620ea")
	)
	(text "No additional decoupling capacitor is needed on the supply line, as one is already present on the \nDisplay module.\n\nCS has a pull-up resistor to keep the Display deselected unless explicitly selected by the MCU.\nThis ensures a defined default state during power-up and reset."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 88.9 23.622 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "6cac1ae9-4192-49b8-9491-76d16808616d")
	)
	(text "nRST circuit generates a signal that is normally \nHIGH (no reset), and LOW when button is pressed \n(reset). the capacitor is used to filter this \nsignal, and in particular avoid noise and EMI to \ntrigger the reset condition."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 226.568 70.866 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "752397ef-8fa9-431e-b2e5-95c031f698d1")
	)
	(text "Flash Memory"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 193.802 16.51 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 2.286 2.286)
				(thickness 0.4572)
				(bold yes)
			)
		)
		(uuid "82aea37e-6da8-4d8f-806b-2358413c0478")
	)
	(text "No additional decoupling capacitor is needed on the supply line, as one is already present on the \nFlash module.\n\nMISO has a series resistor for source termination, which should be placed as close as possible to \nthe line driver. The resistor should only be used if ringing problems occur. Lowering the SPI \nclock frequency can also help reduce ringing effects (not the cause, which is reduced by the \ntermination resistor).\n\nCS has a pull-up resistor to keep the Flash deselected unless explicitly selected by the MCU. \nThis ensures a defined default state during power-up and reset."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 163.576 26.67 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "9b6177bf-83d6-4f29-aa81-5af5fec0a7a2")
	)
	(text "Display"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 121.158 16.51 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 2.286 2.286)
				(thickness 0.4572)
				(bold yes)
			)
		)
		(uuid "9dfeff32-4424-4b6b-ab5a-16a57299ee1c")
	)
	(text "Sensor SPI Interface"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 70.612 229.616 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
				(thickness 0.4572)
				(bold yes)
			)
		)
		(uuid "9f1060a8-156f-46a0-b136-7b1d31f4b085")
	)
	(text "Power Supply"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 349.25 163.83 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 2.286 2.286)
				(thickness 0.4572)
				(bold yes)
			)
		)
		(uuid "b79424ef-7f6c-4ae3-9662-b4900b832821")
	)
	(text "LEDs"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 265.938 16.51 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 2.286 2.286)
				(thickness 0.4572)
				(bold yes)
			)
		)
		(uuid "c18b81cf-7b22-438d-8de4-61db40f5a107")
	)
	(text "User Buttons"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 50.546 16.256 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 2.286 2.286)
				(thickness 0.4572)
				(bold yes)
			)
		)
		(uuid "d0212c5a-8f4a-47ac-9cae-ad50e6dcb2f2")
	)
	(text "The series resistors are the termination resistors for the\nrespective SPI trasmission lines. In general, SPI do not need \ntermination from the standard, but because this connection is \nbetween two different boards, with un-definied cable length in a \nnoisy enviroment, the terminations are used to avoid reflections\nat high SPI frequencies, which can generate ringing \n(particularly critical on CLK line, where it can trigger \nmultiple clock edges. In other lines it is instead sufficient to \nslow down the SPI speed, to let the transmission line settle \nbefore sampling it). \nThese resistors must be mounted as close as possible to the \ndriver to be effective. \nThese terminations, if no ringing is observed during testing, \nshould be substitued with 0 Ohm resistors"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 75.438 240.284 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "e17caccd-876c-4a5d-8e5e-e31d2dcd7bac")
	)
	(text "TVS diode is used to improve the ESD strike resistance of the \nCAN tranciver. It allows to withstand automotive standard ISO \n10605 30kV stikes, in comparison to the 8kV strikes for which \nthe CAN tranciver is rated. \n\nTVS diode must be placed as close as possible the CAN connector \ntoprotect efficiently the CAN transceiver. A direct connection \nto wide GND plane is required to disspate efficiently energy \nfrom the ESD strike with a low impedance return path. \n\nThe TVS is bidirectional to proect against ESD strikes with both \npolarities. It has a 24V working voltage (V_RWM) to avoid \nconducting during normal operations and during possible \nsustained overvoltage conditions (1 CAN line is shorted to 12V \nMain Supply). \nThe clamping voltage (V_CL), the voltage reached during a strike \nis 33V, which is less than the maximum absolute rating of the \ntranciver CAN pins of 42V, ensuring that the strike won't \nactually damage the tranciver.\nThe capacitance of 31pF added on each CAN line is small, \nbasically not degrading the dynamic perfomance of the CAN.\n\n"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 131.064 130.81 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "e1dd0747-7a7d-4ed8-b7b6-3ab4182003b7")
	)
	(text "Class D amplifier is chosen because the application (simple notification, warning and alarm sounds) do not need particular avoidance of distortion, and so\naffordability of the component and low power consumption was prioritized. \nThe nominal power of the amplifier with 8ohm loads (1.2W at 5V) is chosen to be greater than the speaker nominal power (1W). \nAs suggested by the datasheet, the DC component of the Input analog signal is filtered by a series capacitor.\nThe Amplifier is supplied at 5V in order to guarantee that the maximu power output is reachable (with 3V3, the output power would have been impaced because \nideally: Pmax = Vdd^2 / (2*R))\nThe nShutdown pin has a Pull-Down to guarantee the behaviour of the amplifier in Reset conditions (amplifier is shut down unless explicitly waken up by the\nMicrocontroller)"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 306.578 26.67 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "e75e1344-c0b0-4025-a841-01e05cf187bb")
	)
	(text "UART Interface"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 105.664 181.61 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
				(thickness 0.4572)
				(bold yes)
			)
		)
		(uuid "e9da5ccb-d406-4f44-ab4b-d8fd9bc69014")
	)
